# Arithmetic coding replaces symbols with intervals and emits one number
# in (0, 1). Over "Hello World!" it would narrow the interval once per
# character and finally print a point in what remains. The gain is that
# there is no per-symbol code at all; the cost is that the decoder needs
# the same model.
#
# Huffman spends whole bits per symbol, which is why the result above is
# longer than the 96 raw bits for these twelve characters. Arithmetic
# coding wins on long text, where the model learns, and loses here.
